mainmenu "I2C fault injection test"

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_ABSENT_ADDR
	hex "7-bit address that nothing on the bus answers"
	default 0x00
	range 0x00 0x77
	help
	  Address the address-phase conditions transfer to. The default of
	  0x00 is a sentinel meaning "discover one at run time", which scans
	  0x08 through 0x77 and takes the first address that does not answer.

	  Set a value to pin the address instead, which is useful where
	  probing would disturb a device on the bus or where the startup time
	  of the scan matters. Whichever source supplies it, the suite
	  verifies the address is silent before trusting it and fails setup
	  naming it if it answers.

	  This is deliberately not a devicetree property. Devicetree
	  describes what is present on a bus, and this is by definition what
	  is absent.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_PRESENT_ADDR
	hex "7-bit address used for the known-good transfers"
	default 0x00
	range 0x00 0x77
	help
	  Address the suite transfers to when it needs to show that the bus
	  still works. The default of 0x00 means "discover one at run time",
	  which prefers the fault target where one is present and otherwise
	  takes the first address that answers.

	  Only the zero-length probe is ever sent to a discovered address, so
	  an unknown device on the bus is not written to.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_ABSENT_ADDR_10BIT
	hex "10-bit address that nothing on the bus answers"
	default 0x2AA
	range 0x000 0x3FF
	help
	  Address used by the 10-bit addressing condition. The 10-bit space
	  is effectively unpopulated in practice, so a fixed default is
	  defensible here; it is validated by the same probe before use.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_BUS_FAULT_WIRED
	bool "The line-forcing GPIOs are physically jumpered to the bus"
	help
	  Declares that the pins named by sda-gpios and scl-gpios under the
	  zephyr,user node are wired to the controller's own SDA and SCL
	  nets. Set by the twister scenario carrying the i2c_bus_fault
	  fixture.

	  A devicetree property says which pins would be used, not that a
	  jumper is fitted. Without this the hang, recovery and clock-hold
	  conditions skip, because running them against disconnected pins
	  produces failures indistinguishable from driver defects.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_MAX_BUS_TIMEOUTS
	int "Consecutive transfer timeouts before the bus is judged broken"
	default 3
	range 1 100
	help
	  A NACK is an answer and costs nothing. A timeout means the transfer
	  never completed, which is a wiring or bus fault rather than a
	  property of the address, and each one costs
	  CONFIG_I2C_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MS.

	  After this many in a row the suite stops probing and skips the
	  remaining conditions, rather than spending that timeout on every
	  address and every test and reporting results that cannot mean
	  anything.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_RELAXED_ERRNO
	bool "Accept any negative errno from a failed transfer"
	help
	  The I2C API documents -EIO for a failed transfer and the existing
	  upstream suites assert that exact value, so it is what this suite
	  requires by default. Enable this to accept any negative value
	  instead, which is useful when bringing up a driver that is not yet
	  conformant. In both modes the value each driver returned is logged
	  per fault class.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_TIMING_ASSERT
	bool "Fail the run on a timing measurement outside its bounds"
	help
	  Timing measurements are diagnostic by default: they are printed
	  with their expected range and a value outside it is marked in the
	  log but does not fail the run. Enable this to turn them into
	  assertions, which is appropriate when characterising one known
	  board rather than running across many.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_UNSAFE_API_MISUSE
	bool "Run the API misuse conditions that may fault the CPU"
	help
	  Enables the condition that passes a NULL buffer with a non-zero
	  length. Outside userspace the I2C API performs no validation on
	  that path, so a driver is free to dereference the pointer or hand
	  it to a DMA engine, which can fault the CPU and end the run rather
	  than report a result. Off by default for that reason.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Upper bound in milliseconds for a single faulted transfer"
	default 2000
	range 100 30000
	help
	  A transfer that meets a fault must still return within this bound.
	  Transfers run on a worker thread that the test thread waits on with
	  this timeout, so a driver that never returns is reported as a hang
	  rather than exhausting the twister timeout, which is
	  indistinguishable from a build or flash problem.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_WORKER_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack size in bytes for the bounded-transfer worker thread"
	default 2048
	range 512 8192
	help
	  Stack for the thread that faulted transfers run on. It calls
	  nothing deeper than the I2C driver, so the default is generous for
	  most platforms.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_PAYLOAD_LEN
	int "Bytes transferred by the data-phase conditions"
	default 8
	range 4 64
	help
	  Length of the transfers the data-phase conditions use. It must be
	  at least four so that a fault can be placed at the first byte, at a
	  middle byte and at the last byte and land on three distinct
	  positions. It must not exceed the size of the fault target buffer.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_LONG_PAYLOAD_LEN
	int "Bytes transferred by the timing and contention conditions"
	default 32
	range 8 256
	help
	  Length of the transfers used to measure the bus rate and to hold
	  the bus long enough for a second controller to attempt one of its
	  own. Longer is more accurate for the timing measurements and gives
	  a wider contention window, at the cost of run time.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_REPEAT_COUNT
	int "Attempts made by the repeated-fault conditions"
	default 5
	range 2 100
	help
	  Number of times a fault is repeated when checking that a driver
	  does not latch state from the first failure.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_CONTENTION_ROUNDS
	int "Rounds run by the two-controller contention condition"
	default 100
	range 10 1000
	help
	  Number of times two controllers are released onto the bus together
	  to contend for it. Rounds where the two happen to run one after the
	  other produce no arbitration, so more rounds raise the number that
	  do.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_STRESS_CYCLES
	int "Fault-and-recover cycles run by the stress condition"
	default 20
	range 2 500
	help
	  Number of fault-and-recover cycles performed while checking that
	  the driver's state does not degrade. One fault leaves little
	  behind; the failures worth catching accumulate.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_STRETCH_SHORT_US
	int "Clock stretch every driver is expected to tolerate"
	default 200
	range 10 10000
	help
	  Span the fault target holds SCL for in the conditions that must
	  succeed. It is long enough to be measurable against a 100 kHz bus
	  and short enough that no plausible driver times out on it.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_STRETCH_LONG_US
	int "Clock stretch approaching a typical driver timeout"
	default 5000
	range 100 100000
	help
	  Span used by the condition that sits near the boundary. No outcome
	  is required of it, because drivers set their timeouts differently.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_STRETCH_EXCESSIVE_US
	int "Clock stretch beyond any plausible driver timeout"
	default 50000
	range 1000 500000
	help
	  Span used by the condition that must produce a bounded error rather
	  than a hang. The stretch is applied by holding SCL through a GPIO
	  and released by a timer, so a long value costs run time but does not
	  occupy the CPU.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_HOLD_MS
	int "Milliseconds a bus line is held low by the GPIO conditions"
	default 20
	range 1 1000
	help
	  How long SDA or SCL is pulled down through the forcing GPIOs. This
	  is a millisecond-scale operation with no timing requirement: the
	  bus is open drain, so the low wins whatever the controller drives.

config I2C_FAULT_TEST_SCOPE_PAUSE
	bool "Wait for a console keypress before the instrumented conditions"
	select CONSOLE_SUBSYS
	help
	  Halt the run at the start of selected conditions and wait for a
	  character on the console. The condition then runs to completion;
	  there is no second pause.

	  This exists so an oscilloscope can be armed for a single-shot
	  capture at a known point. While the suite waits, no thread is
	  driving the bus, so the first edge after the keypress is the event
	  under investigation and an ordinary edge trigger is unambiguous.
	  Without the pause, the same trigger fires on any of the hundreds of
	  legitimate transfers the run performs first.

	  CONSOLE_GETCHAR must be enabled alongside this symbol. It belongs to
	  an optional choice, which cannot be selected from here, so the two
	  are set together by the scope_pause.conf fragment in this directory.
	  Enabling this symbol without it fails the build rather than silently
	  running without a pause.

	  This makes the suite interactive, so it must not be enabled for an
	  automated run. It is off by default and no scenario in tests.yaml
	  turns it on.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
